      "math_academy_id": "q-15459",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "A circle in the Cartesian plane is given by the equation ${y}^{2}+2x+{x}^{2}=24y-120.$ If the circle is written in the standard form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2},$ then $a+b+r=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the standard form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2},$ we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x+{x}^{2} & =24y-120 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}-24y & =-120 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2x)+({y}^{2}-24y) & =-120 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2}) & =-120+{1}^{2}+{12}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2}) & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-12)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-12)^{2} & ={5}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-1,b=12,r=5,$ and \n\n$$\na+b+r=-1+12+5=16.\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
